= Ecological rationality =

== Overview ==
'''Ecological rationality''' evaluates a decision strategy partly by how well its structure matches the environment in which it is used.

== Core idea ==
A strategy that ignores information or uses a simple heuristic can perform well when environmental regularities make the ignored information unnecessary or noisy. A procedure can therefore be effective without implementing unconstrained optimization.

== Heuristics ==
The fast-and-frugal heuristics program emphasizes the match between simple decision rules and environmental structure. This differs from treating every systematic departure from a normative benchmark as an error.

== Limits ==
Ecological success does not make every heuristic rational. Claims require an explicit environment, objective, comparison class, and performance measure.

== Artificial systems ==
For artificial cognition, the concept suggests evaluating model tendencies against deployment environments rather than labeling every stable tendency a defect. This is an analogy at the computational level, not evidence that human and machine mechanisms are identical.

== References ==
* Gerd Gigerenzer, Peter M. Todd, and the ABC Research Group, ''Simple Heuristics That Make Us Smart'' (1999).
* Gerd Gigerenzer & Henry Brighton, “Homo Heuristicus”, ''Topics in Cognitive Science'' 1 (2009): 107–143.
* “Bounded Rationality”, ''Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy''.

